"""Draft for Required Course Book 3, Chapter 10 (Energy in an Electrostatic Field) and Chapter 11 (Circuits and Their Applications), PEP 2019 edition."""
from phy_hs_lib import T, C, N

# ======================= Chapter 10: Energy in an Electrostatic Field =======================
T("electric_pe", "Energy", "conceptual",
  "Work by the electrostatic force depends only on a charge's initial and final positions, not on its path, so electric potential energy can be defined. Work by the electrostatic force equals the decrease in potential energy: W_AB = E_pA − E_pB. The value of potential energy depends on the chosen zero level, but its change does not.",
  ["Can determine the sign of electrostatic work and use it to identify changes in potential energy", "Can calculate using W_AB = E_pA − E_pB and W = qEd", "Know that electric potential energy is shared by the charge and field and is relative"],
  [("B3.1.5", "exact")],
  pre=[("work", "hard"), ("e_field", "hard"), ("gravity_pe", "soft")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "In the electric field of an isolated positive point charge Q, a positive test charge moves from point A, closer to Q, to point B, farther away. Which statement describes the electrostatic work and change in potential energy?",
      "The electrostatic force does positive work, and potential energy decreases", ["The electrostatic force does negative work, and potential energy increases", "The electrostatic force does positive work, and potential energy increases", "The electrostatic force does negative work, and potential energy decreases"],
      "Like charges repel. As the positive test charge moves away from Q, the angle between the electrostatic force and displacement is less than 90°, so the force does positive work and potential energy decreases. Mistaking “moving away” for “working against the electrostatic force” is a common error."),
    N("basic", "A charge has electric potential energy 0.8 mJ at point A. It moves to point B while 0.3 mJ of work is done against the electrostatic force. What is its potential energy at B, in mJ? (Enter a number only.)", "1.1",
      "Work done against the electrostatic force means the force does negative work, W_AB = −0.3 mJ. From W_AB = E_pA − E_pB, E_pB = E_pA − W_AB = 0.8 + 0.3 = 1.1 mJ. Treating the work against the force as a decrease in potential energy would give 0.5.",
      "0.8+0.3"),
    C("standard", "Which statement about electric potential energy is correct?",
      "Its value depends on the chosen zero level, but its change does not",
      ["A charge's potential energy at any point in an electric field must be positive", "When the electrostatic force does positive work, the charge's potential energy must increase", "If the electric field is zero where the charge is located, its potential energy must be zero"],
      "Potential energy is relative. Different choices of zero give different values at each point, but the difference between two points (the work by the electrostatic force) is unchanged. Potential energy can be positive or negative; positive electrostatic work decreases it; and zero potential energy is not necessarily associated with a zero field."),
    N("advanced", "A uniform electric field has strength E = 2×10³ N/C. A point charge q = −5×10⁻⁶ C moves 0.2 m along a field line. By how many J does its electric potential energy increase? (Enter a number only.)", "0.002",
      "The electrostatic force on a negative charge is opposite the field line, so the charge moves against the force and the force does negative work. Its magnitude is W = |q|Ed = 5×10⁻⁶ × 2×10³ × 0.2 = 2×10⁻³ J, so potential energy increases by 0.002 J. Assuming the force does positive work would incorrectly predict a decrease in potential energy.",
      "5e-6*2e3*0.2"),
  ])

T("potential", "Motion and Interaction", "conceptual",
  "The electric potential at a point is the electric potential energy of a charge there divided by its charge, φ = E_p/q. Potential is a scalar with a signed value. It decreases along an electric field line, and its value depends on the chosen zero-potential point.",
  ["Understand the definition φ = E_p/q and know that potential is determined by the field, not by the test charge", "Can determine how potential changes along a field line and compare potentials in a point-charge field", "Know that potential is relative and can convert values after changing the zero-potential point"],
  [("B3.1.5", "exact")],
  pre=[("electric_pe", "hard"), ("e_field", "soft")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "Which statement about the electric potential at a point in an electric field is correct?",
      "Potential is a scalar, and its value depends on the chosen zero-potential point",
      ["Potential is a vector pointing in the direction of the electric field at that point", "Potential is proportional to the charge of the test charge placed there", "Potential must be zero at a point where the electric field is zero"],
      "φ = E_p/q is a ratio definition. Potential is determined by the field and the zero-potential point, not by the test charge, and it is a scalar. Potential and field strength are independent quantities: potential need not be zero where the field is zero (for example, at the midpoint between two equal like charges)."),
    N("basic", "A positive point charge q = 2×10⁻⁸ C at point A in an electric field has potential energy 6×10⁻⁶ J. Take the potential at infinity to be zero. What is the potential at A, in V? (Enter a number only.)", "300",
      "φ_A = E_p/q = 6×10⁻⁶ ÷ 2×10⁻⁸ = 300 V. Using a test charge with a larger charge would increase its potential energy proportionally, leaving the potential unchanged.",
      "6e-6/2e-8"),
    C("standard", "Take the potential at infinity to be zero. In the field of an isolated negative point charge −Q, how does the potential change as a point gets closer to −Q?",
      "It decreases and is negative", ["It increases and is positive", "It decreases but remains positive", "It is the same at every point and equals zero"],
      "Field lines point from infinity toward −Q, and potential decreases along a field line. Near −Q the potential is below zero and becomes more negative as the distance decreases."),
    N("standard", "The potential at A is 8 V and at B is 20 V. If A is chosen as the zero-potential point, what is the potential at B, in V? (Enter a number only.)", "12",
      "The potential difference φ_B − φ_A = 12 V is independent of the zero-potential point. With A as zero, φ_A′ = 0, so φ_B′ = 12 V. Assuming potential itself is unchanged would give 20.",
      "20-8"),
  ])

T("potential_diff", "Motion and Interaction", "procedural",
  "The potential difference between two points is U_AB = φ_A − φ_B, and U_AB = −U_BA. Work by the electrostatic force is related to potential difference by W_AB = qU_AB. Potential difference is a signed scalar, independent of the chosen zero-potential point.",
  ["Can calculate using U_AB = φ_A − φ_B and W_AB = qU_AB, including the signs of q and W", "Know that potential difference is independent of the zero-potential point and that U_AB = −U_BA", "Can compare two potentials from the sign of their potential difference"],
  [("B3.1.5", "exact")],
  pre=[("potential", "hard"), ("electric_pe", "hard")],
  qs=[
    N("basic", "A positive charge q = 2×10⁻⁶ C moves from A to B in an electric field, and the electrostatic force does 6×10⁻⁴ J of work. What is the potential difference U_AB, in V? (Enter a number only.)", "300",
      "U_AB = W_AB/q = 6×10⁻⁴ ÷ 2×10⁻⁶ = 300 V. The positive value means the potential at A is 300 V higher than at B.",
      "6e-4/2e-6"),
    C("basic", "Which statement about potential difference is correct?",
      "It is independent of the chosen zero-potential point, and U_AB = −U_BA",
      ["Its value depends on the chosen zero-potential point", "U_AB = W_AB/q means U_AB is proportional to W_AB and inversely proportional to q", "Potential difference is a vector, and U_AB points from A to B"],
      "Potential difference is the difference between two potentials and is independent of the zero-potential point. It is a scalar; its sign indicates which point has higher potential. U_AB is determined by the field and the positions of A and B. W_AB/q is a way to calculate it, not evidence that U_AB changes with q."),
    N("standard", "A point charge q = −4×10⁻⁶ C moves from A to B, and 8×10⁻⁴ J of work is done against the electrostatic force. What is U_AB, in V? (Enter a number only.)", "200",
      "Work done against the electrostatic force means W_AB = −8×10⁻⁴ J. Including the signs, U_AB = W_AB/q = (−8×10⁻⁴)/(−4×10⁻⁶) = 200 V. Treating the work against the force as W = +8×10⁻⁴ J would give −200 V and reverse the comparison of the potentials at A and B.",
      "(-8e-4)/(-4e-6)"),
    C("standard", "An electron (charge −1.6×10⁻¹⁹ C) moves from A to B, and the electrostatic force does W_AB = 4×10⁻¹⁹ J of work. What is U_AB?",
      "-2.5 V", ["2.5 V", "-4 V", "-0.4 V"],
      "U_AB = W_AB/q = 4×10⁻¹⁹ ÷ (−1.6×10⁻¹⁹) = −2.5 V, so the potential at A is 2.5 V lower than at B. Forgetting the electron's negative charge gives +2.5 V; ignoring the charge and using only the coefficient of W gives −4 V.",
      verify="4e-19/(-1.6e-19)"),
  ])

T("equipotential", "Motion and Interaction", "conceptual",
  "A surface of points with the same electric potential is an equipotential surface. The electrostatic force does no work when a charge moves along one. Electric field lines are perpendicular to equipotential surfaces and point from higher to lower potential. If adjacent equipotential surfaces have equal potential differences, the field is stronger where the surfaces are closer together.",
  ["Know that field lines are perpendicular to equipotential surfaces and that the electrostatic force does no work along one", "Can compare field strengths from equipotential spacing and potentials from field-line direction", "Can calculate the work done by the electrostatic force when a charge moves between equipotential surfaces using W = qU"],
  [("B3.1.5", "exact")],
  pre=[("potential_diff", "hard"), ("field_lines", "hard")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "Which statement about equipotential surfaces is correct?",
      "Electric field lines are perpendicular to equipotential surfaces, and the electrostatic force does no work when a charge moves along one",
      ["The electric field strength is necessarily the same at every point on an equipotential surface", "Electric field lines always run tangent to equipotential surfaces", "The electrostatic force always does positive work when a charge moves along an equipotential surface"],
      "Potential is the same everywhere on an equipotential surface, so the electrostatic force does no work as a charge moves along it. This means the force (and field) is perpendicular to the surface, as are the field lines. Field strength need not be equal at all points on the same equipotential surface, such as on the perpendicular bisector of two equal and opposite charges."),
    C("basic", "The drawn equipotential surfaces have equal potential differences between adjacent surfaces. If they are closer together at A than at B, which statement is correct?",
      "The electric field is stronger at A than at B", ["The electric field is weaker at A than at B", "The potential at A must be higher than at B", "The field strengths at A and B are equal"],
      "With equal potential differences between adjacent surfaces, a smaller spacing gives a larger E ≈ U/d, so the field is stronger where the surfaces are closer. Potential ordering depends on field-line direction, not on equipotential spacing."),
    N("standard", "Two equipotential surfaces have potentials 30 V and −10 V. A positive charge q = 2×10⁻⁶ C moves from the first surface to the second. How much work, in J, does the electrostatic force do? (Enter a number only.)", "0.00008",
      "U = 30 − (−10) = 40 V, and W = qU = 2×10⁻⁶ × 40 = 8×10⁻⁵ J = 0.00008 J. Using 30 − 10 = 20 V for the potential difference would give the incorrect value 4×10⁻⁵ J.",
      "2e-6*40"),
    N("advanced", "An electron moves only under the electrostatic force. It has kinetic energy 5 eV when it passes an equipotential surface at −10 V, then moves to a surface at 10 V. What is its kinetic energy there, in eV? (Enter a number only.)", "25",
      "As the electron moves from lower to higher potential, the electrostatic force does positive work: W = qU = (−e)(−10 − 10) V = 20 eV. By the work-energy theorem, E_k = 5 + 20 = 25 eV. Treating the electron as a positive charge would give 5 − 20 < 0, an impossible result.",
      "5+20"),
  ])

T("u_e_relation", "Motion and Interaction", "procedural",
  "In a uniform electric field, the potential difference between two points along the field is the product of field strength and their separation in that direction: U = Ed, or E = U/d. This formula applies only to uniform fields, and d is the distance along the field. In a nonuniform field, it can be used to compare potential differences qualitatively.",
  ["Can use U = Ed to calculate potential difference or field strength in a uniform field", "Understand that d is measured along the field and handle a line between two points at an angle to the field", "Can qualitatively compare potential differences over equal distances in a nonuniform field using E"],
  [("B3.1.5", "exact")],
  pre=[("potential_diff", "hard"), ("e_field", "hard")],
  math=[("cnm_h060", "When the line between two points makes an angle θ with the field, the distance along the field is d = AB·cosθ.")],
  qs=[
    N("basic", "In a uniform electric field, two points are 4 cm apart along the field. What is their potential difference, in V, if E = 500 V/m? (Enter a number only.)", "20",
      "U = Ed = 500 × 0.04 = 20 V. Convert cm to m; using 4 directly would give 2000.",
      "500*0.04"),
    C("basic", "Which statement about E = U/d is correct?",
      "It applies to a uniform electric field, and d is the distance between the points along the field",
      ["It applies to every electric field, and d is the distance between the points", "In a uniform field, a larger U must mean a larger E", "d is the length of any path between the points"],
      "U = Ed follows from the constant electrostatic force in a uniform field, so d must be measured along the field. For the same U, different values of d give different E; therefore E cannot be determined from U alone."),
    N("standard", "In a uniform electric field, points A and B are 10 cm apart, and the line AB makes a 60° angle with the field. If E = 200 V/m, what is the magnitude of the potential difference between A and B, in V? (Enter a number only.)", "10",
      "The distance along the field is d = AB·cos60° = 0.1 × 0.5 = 0.05 m, so U = Ed = 200 × 0.05 = 10 V. Using 0.1 m directly would give 20 V; using sin60° would give about 17.3 V.",
      "200*0.1*0.5"),
    C("standard", "Points A, B, and C lie in that order on the same field line of an isolated positive point charge. B is the midpoint of AC, and A is closer to the charge. How do |U_AB| and |U_BC| compare?",
      "U_AB > U_BC", ["U_AB = U_BC", "U_AB < U_BC", "Their magnitudes cannot be compared"],
      "The field is stronger closer to the point charge, so the average field over AB is greater than over BC. Since AB = BC, U_AB ≈ E̅_AB·d > E̅_BC·d ≈ U_BC. Concluding U_AB = U_BC incorrectly applies U = Ed to a nonuniform field."),
  ])

T("capacitor", "Motion and Interaction", "conceptual",
  "A capacitor consists of two conductors separated by an insulator. Its capacitance C = Q/U describes its ability to store charge and is determined by its structure. For a parallel-plate capacitor, capacitance is proportional to the facing plate area and relative permittivity ε_r, and inversely proportional to the plate separation: C = ε_rS/(4πkd).",
  ["Can use C = Q/U to calculate capacitance or charge", "Know that capacitance depends on the capacitor's structure, not on Q or U", "Can analyze changes in C, Q, U, and E for a parallel-plate capacitor when voltage or charge is held constant"],
  [("B3.1.6", "exact")],
  pre=[("potential_diff", "hard"), ("charge", "soft")],
  math=[("cnm_238", "The capacitance of a parallel-plate capacitor is inversely proportional to the plate separation d, which can be understood from the graph of an inverse-proportion function.")],
  qs=[
    N("basic", "A capacitor has charge 4 μC when the potential difference between its plates is 20 V. What is its capacitance, in μF? (Enter a number only.)", "0.2",
      "C = Q/U = 4 μC ÷ 20 V = 0.2 μF. μC and μF have the same scale factor, so there is no need to convert to C and back.",
      "4/20"),
    C("basic", "Which statement about a capacitor's capacitance is correct?",
      "Capacitance is determined by the capacitor's structure, not by its charge or the voltage between its plates",
      ["capacitance is proportional to charge", "capacitance is inversely proportional to the voltage between the plates", "an uncharged capacitor has zero capacitance"],
      "C = Q/U is a ratio definition, similar to ρ = m/V. Capacitance depends on the facing plate area, plate separation, and dielectric; it exists even when the capacitor is uncharged."),
    C("standard", "A charged parallel-plate capacitor is disconnected from the power supply, then the distance d between its plates is increased. Which statement is correct?",
      "Q stays constant, C decreases, U increases, and the field E between the plates stays constant",
      ["Q stays constant, C decreases, U stays constant, and E decreases", "Q decreases, C decreases, U stays constant, and E decreases", "Q stays constant, C increases, U decreases, and E increases"],
      "After the supply is disconnected, charge Q remains constant. Increasing d decreases C = ε_rS/(4πkd); from U = Q/C, U increases. E = U/d = 4πkQ/(ε_rS) does not depend on d, so E remains constant. The conclusion “Q decreases, C decreases, U stays constant, and E decreases” applies when the capacitor remains connected to the supply, but here it is disconnected, so Q cannot change."),
    N("standard", "A parallel-plate capacitor with capacitance C = 0.5 μF stays connected to a 20 V supply. A dielectric is inserted between the plates, tripling the capacitance. By how many μC does the charge increase? (Enter a number only.)", "20",
      "With the supply connected, U remains 20 V. Q₁ = CU = 0.5 × 20 = 10 μC, Q₂ = 3C·U = 30 μC, so ΔQ = 20 μC. Assuming Q remains constant would give an increase of 0.",
      "0.5*3*20-0.5*20"),
  ])

T("exp_capacitor", "Motion and Interaction", "procedural",
  "Connect a capacitor to a power supply, resistor, and current sensor, then observe how current and voltage change during charging and discharging. Current starts high and decreases. The area under the I-t graph gives the capacitor's charge, and C = Q/U can be used to estimate capacitance.",
  ["Can describe how current and voltage change while a capacitor charges and discharges", "Can estimate charge by counting grid squares under an I-t graph and use it to find capacitance", "Can analyze how the I-t graph changes when conditions such as resistance change"],
  [("B3.X.1", "exact"), ("B3.1.6", "inferred")],
  pre=[("capacitor", "hard"), ("potential_diff", "soft")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "A capacitor begins charging through a resistor from a constant-voltage supply. Which statement about the charging process is correct?",
      "The charging current decreases to zero, while the voltage between the plates rises to the supply voltage",
      ["The charging current increases from zero to a maximum", "The charging current stays constant until the capacitor is charged", "A constant current continues after charging, and the capacitor voltage keeps increasing"],
      "Immediately after connection, the capacitor voltage is zero, the resistor has the full supply voltage across it, and current is at its maximum. As capacitor voltage rises, the voltage across the resistor and the current decrease. When U_C equals the supply voltage, current is zero."),
    C("basic", "In an experiment, a capacitor is charged and then discharged through the same resistor. How does the current through resistor R flow during charging and discharging?",
      "in opposite directions", ["in the same direction", "current flows while charging but not while discharging", "the discharge current's direction depends on the capacitor's charge magnitude"],
      "During charging, positive charge flows from the positive terminal of the supply to the connected plate. During discharge, positive charge on that plate flows through the resistor back to the other plate, so the current through the resistor reverses."),
    N("standard", "In a capacitor-discharge experiment, an I-t graph is recorded. The area under the curve covers about 40 small squares, each representing 0.2 mA × 0.5 s. The charging voltage was 8 V. What is the capacitor's approximate capacitance, in μF? (Enter a number only.)", "500",
      "Each square represents charge 0.2 mA × 0.5 s = 0.1 mC = 100 μC. Total charge is Q = 40 × 100 = 4000 μC. C = Q/U = 4000 ÷ 8 = 500 μF. Unit conversion is a common source of error (mA·s = mC).",
      "40*0.2*0.5*1000/8"),
    C("standard", "In a discharge experiment, the resistor R is replaced with one of greater resistance, with all other conditions unchanged. How does the discharge I-t graph change?",
      "The initial current decreases, the discharge takes longer, and the area under the curve stays the same",
      ["The initial current decreases, the discharge takes less time, and the area under the curve decreases", "The initial current increases, the discharge takes less time, and the area stays the same", "The initial current stays the same, the discharge takes longer, and the area increases"],
      "At the start of discharge, I₀ = U/R, so a larger R gives a smaller I₀. The total charge released by the capacitor, Q = CU, is unchanged, so the area stays the same. With lower current, it takes longer to release the charge."),
  ])

T("particle_accel", "Energy", "application",
  "Ignoring gravity, a charged particle accelerated by an electric field obeys the work-energy relation qU = ½mv² − ½mv₀², which can be used to find its final speed or kinetic energy. This relation applies to both uniform and nonuniform fields and depends on potential difference, not plate separation.",
  ["Can use qU = ΔE_k to analyze a charged particle's speed and kinetic energy in an accelerating field", "Know that the final speed is proportional to √(q/m) and can compare speeds of different particles", "Know that qU = ΔE_k applies in both uniform and nonuniform fields"],
  [("B3.1.5", "exact")],
  pre=[("potential_diff", "hard"), ("work_energy_theorem", "hard"), ("newton2", "soft")],
  qs=[
    N("basic", "An α particle (charge 2e) starts from rest and is accelerated through a potential difference of 500 V. What kinetic energy does it gain, in eV? (Enter a number only.)", "1000",
      "qU = 2e × 500 V = 1000 eV, so the kinetic energy is 1000 eV. One eV is the kinetic energy gained by a particle with charge e accelerated through 1 V. Forgetting that the particle's charge is 2e would give 500.",
      "2*500"),
    C("basic", "A proton (charge e, mass m) and an α particle (charge 2e, mass 4m) start from rest and are accelerated through the same potential difference. What is the ratio of their final speeds, v_proton : v_α?",
      "√2 : 1", ["1 : √2", "2 : 1", "1 : 2"],
      "From qU = ½mv², v = √(2qU/m) and v ∝ √(q/m). For the proton, q/m = e/m; for the α particle, q/m = 2e/4m = e/(2m). Thus v_proton : v_α = √2 : 1. Their kinetic-energy ratio, by contrast, is 1 : 2."),
    N("standard", "A charged particle (gravity neglected) has q = 2×10⁻⁶ C and m = 1×10⁻⁶ kg. Starting from rest, it is accelerated through U = 100 V. What speed does it gain, in m/s? (Enter a number only.)", "20",
      "qU = ½mv², so v = √(2qU/m) = √(2 × 2×10⁻⁶ × 100 ÷ 1×10⁻⁶) = √400 = 20 m/s. Omitting the factor of 2 would give about 14.1 m/s.",
      "sqrt(2*2e-6*100/1e-6)"),
    C("standard", "A charged particle (gravity neglected) starts from rest and is accelerated by a uniform field between two parallel plates, reaching the other plate. The voltage stays constant while the plate separation doubles. How does the particle's speed at the other plate change?",
      "it stays the same", ["it becomes √2 times the original", "it doubles", "it becomes 1/√2 of the original"],
      "qU = ½mv² depends on the potential difference U. Since U is unchanged, v is unchanged. Increasing d reduces field strength and acceleration, but the particle travels a greater distance in the field; the effects cancel in the work-energy theorem."),
  ])

T("particle_deflect", "Motion and Interaction", "modeling",
  "When a charged particle enters a uniform electric field perpendicular to the field direction, it follows a projectile-like path: uniform motion perpendicular to the field, x = v₀t, and uniformly accelerated motion from rest along the field, y = ½at², where a = qE/m = qU/(md). The deflection angle satisfies tanθ = v_y/v₀.",
  ["Can decompose deflection into uniform motion perpendicular to the field and uniformly accelerated motion along it", "Can calculate the displacement y and tanθ of the deflection angle", "Know that after acceleration through the same field, deflection is independent of the particle's q and m"],
  [("B3.1.5", "exact")],
  pre=[("particle_accel", "hard"), ("projectile", "hard"), ("u_e_relation", "soft")],
  math=[("cnm_230", "Along the field, both y = ½at² and y = ax² have parabolic graphs."), ("cnm_h060", "The tangent of the deflection angle, tanθ = v_y/v₀, uses the definition of a trigonometric ratio.")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "A charged particle (gravity neglected) enters a uniform electric field with initial velocity v₀ perpendicular to the field. What is its trajectory?",
      "a parabola", ["a circular arc", "a straight line", "an ellipse"],
      "There is no force perpendicular to the field, so the particle moves uniformly in that direction. Along the field it experiences a constant electrostatic force and uniform acceleration. The combined motion is like projectile motion, so the trajectory is parabolic. Circular motion requires a constant-magnitude force that always points toward the center."),
    N("basic", "A charged particle (gravity neglected) enters a uniform electric field of length L = 0.1 m at v₀ = 2×10⁶ m/s, perpendicular to the field. Its acceleration in the field is 4×10¹² m/s². How far, in mm, is it deflected along the field when it exits? (Enter a number only.)", "5",
      "t = L/v₀ = 0.1 ÷ 2×10⁶ = 5×10⁻⁸ s. Then y = ½at² = ½ × 4×10¹² × (5×10⁻⁸)² = 5×10⁻³ m = 5 mm. Omitting ½ would give 10 mm.",
      "0.5*4e12*(0.1/2e6)**2*1000"),
    N("standard", "Using the same conditions, a particle enters a uniform electric field of length L = 0.1 m at v₀ = 2×10⁶ m/s, perpendicular to the field, with acceleration 4×10¹² m/s². What is tanθ, where θ is the angle between its velocity at exit and its initial velocity? (Enter a number only.)", "0.1",
      "t = 5×10⁻⁸ s, so v_y = at = 4×10¹² × 5×10⁻⁸ = 2×10⁵ m/s. Thus tanθ = v_y/v₀ = 2×10⁵ ÷ 2×10⁶ = 0.1. Note that tanθ = 2y/L (for y = 5 mm, this is 0.1), not y/L.",
      "4e12*(0.1/2e6)/2e6"),
    C("advanced", "A proton and an α particle (twice the proton's charge and four times its mass) start from rest, are accelerated by the same field, then enter and exit the same uniform deflection field perpendicularly. How do their deflections y compare?",
      "they have the same deflection and follow the same path", ["the α particle's deflection is half the proton's", "the α particle is deflected more because its charge is greater", "the proton is deflected more because its mass is smaller"],
      "For acceleration, qU₁ = ½mv₀². For deflection, y = ½(qU₂/md)(L/v₀)² = U₂L²/(4dU₁), independent of q and m. Comparing only accelerations (the α particle's acceleration is half the proton's) and ignoring their different speeds leads to the incorrect conclusion “half.”"),
  ])

# ======================= Chapter 11: Circuits and Their Applications =======================
T("current", "Energy", "conceptual",
  "Electric current is the directed motion of charge, with I = q/t. In a metal conductor, its microscopic expression is I = nqSv. Current is defined in the direction positive charges move. A power supply maintains a potential difference across a conductor so that current continues to flow.",
  ["Can use I = q/t to calculate current and know how its direction is defined", "Can use I = nqSv to estimate the drift speed of free electrons", "Know the purpose of a power supply and handle current when positive and negative charges move simultaneously"],
  [("B3.2.1", "inferred")],
  pre=[("charge", "hard"), ("J:circuit", "soft"), ("e_field", "soft")],
  qs=[
    N("basic", "A charge of 12 C passes through a cross-section of a conductor in 30 s. What is the current, in A? (Enter a number only.)", "0.4",
      "I = q/t = 12 ÷ 30 = 0.4 A.",
      "12/30"),
    C("basic", "Which statement correctly describes the role of a power supply?",
      "It maintains a potential difference across a conductor so that current continues to flow",
      ["it provides the conductor with a large supply of freely moving charges", "it stops the random thermal motion of free charges in the conductor", "it creates charge and sends it from the negative terminal to the positive terminal"],
      "A conductor already contains many free charges. Sustained current requires a sustained potential difference (electric field); the power supply uses non-electrostatic forces to move positive charge from the negative terminal to the positive terminal and maintain that difference. Charge is neither created nor destroyed."),
    N("standard", "A copper wire has cross-sectional area S = 1 mm², free-electron density n = 8.5×10²⁸ m⁻³, and electron charge magnitude e = 1.6×10⁻¹⁹ C. If its current is I = 3.4 A, what is the electron drift speed in mm/s? (Enter a number only.)", "0.25",
      "From I = nevS, v = I/(neS) = 3.4 ÷ (8.5×10²⁸ × 1.6×10⁻¹⁹ × 1×10⁻⁶) = 2.5×10⁻⁴ m/s = 0.25 mm/s. Convert the area to m². This shows that electron drift speed is very small, far below the speed of thermal motion.",
      "round(3.4/(8.5e28*1.6e-19*1e-6)*1000,2)"),
    C("standard", "Across a cross-section of an electrolyte in 1 s, 2 C of positive ions move right while 2 C of negative ions move left. What is the current through the cross-section during that time?",
      "4 A to the right", ["2 A to the right", "0", "4 A to the left"],
      "Negative ions moving left are equivalent to positive charge moving right, so both ion flows contribute current in the same direction. Their magnitudes add: I = (2 + 2) C ÷ 1 s = 4 A to the right. Subtracting the charges to get 0 incorrectly treats the negative ions' opposite motion as charge neutralization; counting only one kind of ion gives 2 A."),
  ])

T("ohm_law", "Energy", "conceptual",
  "Current through a conductor is proportional to the voltage across it and inversely proportional to its resistance: I = U/R. The I-U graph for a linear component is a straight line through the origin; for a nonlinear component it is a curve, but the resistance at any state is still R = U/I. Ohm's law applies to metallic conduction and electrolytic solutions.",
  ["Can use I = U/R to calculate current, voltage, and resistance", "Can read an I-U graph, distinguish linear from nonlinear components, and find resistance at a given state", "Know the scope of Ohm's law"],
  [("B3.2.2", "inferred")],
  pre=[("current", "hard"), ("J:ohm", "hard")],
  math=[("cnm_200", "The I-U graph of a linear component is a direct-proportion line through the origin with slope 1/R.")],
  qs=[
    N("basic", "A metal conductor carries 0.5 A when the voltage across it is 4 V. If its temperature stays constant and the voltage increases to 10 V, what is the current, in A? (Enter a number only.)", "1.25",
      "R = U/I = 4 ÷ 0.5 = 8 Ω. At constant temperature, R is unchanged, so I′ = U′/R = 10 ÷ 8 = 1.25 A. Alternatively, use direct proportionality: I′ = 0.5 × 10/4.",
      "10/(4/0.5)"),
    C("basic", "The I-U graph for a small bulb curves toward the U-axis, with a decreasing slope. What does this show?",
      "the filament's resistance increases as its temperature rises", ["the filament's resistance decreases as voltage rises", "the filament's resistance is constant and the ammeter is inaccurate", "R = U/I is no longer meaningful"],
      "Higher voltage heats the filament, increasing the metal's resistivity and its resistance R = U/I. The slope of a line from the origin to a point on the I-U graph (1/R) decreases. Resistance at every point on the curve can still be found using R = U/I."),
    N("standard", "A small bulb carries 0.25 A at 2 V and 0.5 A at 6 V. By how many Ω does its filament resistance increase as the voltage rises from 2 V to 6 V? (Enter a number only.)", "4",
      "R₁ = 2 ÷ 0.25 = 8 Ω, R₂ = 6 ÷ 0.5 = 12 Ω, so ΔR = 4 Ω. Using ΔU/ΔI = 4 ÷ 0.25 = 16 Ω as the change in resistance incorrectly treats a nonlinear component as linear.",
      "6/0.5-2/0.25"),
    C("standard", "To which conductors does Ohm's law apply?",
      "metallic conductors and electrolytic solutions", ["all conductors under all conditions", "metallic conductors but not electrolytic solutions", "gaseous conductors and semiconductor diodes"],
      "Ohm's law applies to metallic and electrolytic conduction, but not to gaseous conduction or semiconductor devices such as diodes and transistors."),
  ])

T("resistance", "Energy", "procedural",
  "A conductor's resistance is proportional to its length and inversely proportional to its cross-sectional area: R = ρ·l/S. Resistivity ρ is a property of the material, changes with temperature, and is independent of the conductor's length and cross-sectional area.",
  ["Can use R = ρl/S to calculate resistance", "Can analyze resistance changes when a wire is folded or uniformly stretched at constant volume", "Can distinguish resistivity from resistance and know that metal resistivity increases with temperature"],
  [("B3.2.2", "exact")],
  pre=[("ohm_law", "hard"), ("J:resistance", "soft")],
  math=[("cnm_238", "R is inversely proportional to S and directly proportional to l; inverse and direct proportion can be used to reason about it.")],
  qs=[
    N("basic", "Copper has resistivity ρ = 1.7×10⁻⁸ Ω·m. What is the resistance, in Ω, of a 100 m copper wire with cross-sectional area 1.7 mm²? (Enter a number only.)", "1",
      "S = 1.7 mm² = 1.7×10⁻⁶ m², and R = ρl/S = 1.7×10⁻⁸ × 100 ÷ 1.7×10⁻⁶ = 1 Ω. Failing to convert mm² to m² would cause an error by a factor of 10⁶.",
      "round(1.7e-8*100/1.7e-6,2)"),
    C("basic", "A uniform resistance wire has resistance R. It is folded in half and twisted into a single strand. What is its new resistance?",
      "1/4 of the original", ["1/2 of the original", "twice the original", "4 times the original"],
      "After folding, the length becomes l/2 and the cross-sectional area becomes 2S, so R′ = ρ(l/2)/(2S) = R/4. Considering only the halved length would give R/2."),
    N("standard", "A uniform resistance wire has resistance 4 Ω. It is stretched uniformly to 3 times its original length, with volume unchanged. What is its new resistance, in Ω? (Enter a number only.)", "36",
      "With volume unchanged, tripling the length reduces the cross-sectional area to 1/3. R′ = ρ(3l)/(S/3) = 9ρl/S = 9R = 36 Ω. Considering only the change in length would give 12 Ω.",
      "4*9"),
    C("standard", "Which statement about resistivity is correct?",
      "Resistivity is determined by the material and changes with temperature; it is independent of wire length and cross-sectional area",
      ["resistivity is proportional to conductor length", "resistivity is proportional to conductor resistance", "the resistivity of a metal decreases as temperature rises"],
      "In R = ρl/S, ρ is a material property, while R depends on l and S. The resistivity of a pure metal increases with temperature (some alloys change very little)."),
  ])

T("exp_length", "Interdisciplinary Practice", "procedural",
  "A vernier caliper reading is the main-scale reading plus the aligned vernier division multiplied by the resolution: 10 divisions give 0.1 mm, 20 divisions give 0.05 mm, and 50 divisions give 0.02 mm. Do not estimate an extra digit. A micrometer has 0.01 mm resolution; its reading is the sleeve reading plus the thimble reading × 0.01 mm, with one estimated digit.",
  ["Can read 10-, 20-, and 50-division vernier calipers in mm without estimating an extra digit", "Can read a micrometer, including the half-millimeter mark and estimated digit", "Can judge whether a reading is reasonable for the instrument's resolution and choose an appropriate tool"],
  [("B3.X.2", "exact"), ("B3.2.2", "inferred")],
  pre=[("J:len_time", "hard"), ("resistance", "soft")],
  qs=[
    N("basic", "A 10-division vernier caliper measures an object. The main-scale reading is 23 mm, and the 6th vernier mark aligns with a mark on the main scale. What is the object's length, in mm? (Enter a number only.)", "23.6",
      "A 10-division vernier caliper has 0.1 mm resolution, so the reading is 23 + 6 × 0.1 = 23.6 mm. No extra digit is estimated with a vernier caliper.",
      "round(23+6*0.1,1)"),
    C("basic", "A 20-division vernier caliper has 0.05 mm resolution. Which of these readings is possible?",
      "23.45 mm", ["23.46 mm", "23.47 mm", "23.48 mm"],
      "A 20-division vernier reading is the whole-millimeter main-scale value plus n × 0.05 mm. Its decimal part can only be 0.00, 0.05, 0.10, 0.15, ...; the last digit must be 0 or 5. The final digits in 23.46, 23.47, and 23.48 are impossible (23.46 can be obtained only with a 50-division caliper).",
      verify="round(23+9*0.05,2)"),
    N("standard", "A 50-division vernier caliper measures a small cylinder's diameter. The main-scale reading is 12 mm, and the 23rd vernier mark aligns with a main-scale mark. What is the cylinder's diameter, in mm? (Enter a number only.)", "12.46",
      "A 50-division caliper has 0.02 mm resolution, so the reading is 12 + 23 × 0.02 = 12.46 mm. Using 0.1 mm resolution by mistake would give 14.3 mm.",
      "round(12+23*0.02,2)"),
    N("standard", "A micrometer measures a wire's diameter. The sleeve reading is 3.5 mm (the half-millimeter mark is visible), and the thimble reading is 21.7 divisions, including one estimated digit; each division is 0.01 mm. What is the wire's diameter, in mm? (Enter a number only.)", "3.717",
      "Reading = sleeve + thimble × 0.01 mm = 3.5 + 21.7 × 0.01 = 3.717 mm. A micrometer requires estimating to 0.001 mm. Missing the visible half-millimeter mark would omit 0.5 mm and give 3.217.",
      "round(3.5+21.7*0.01,3)"),
  ])

T("exp_resistivity", "Energy", "procedural",
  "The resistivity of a wire is measured using ρ = RS/l = πd²R/(4l): measure diameter d with a micrometer, the effective circuit length l with a ruler, and resistance R using the voltmeter-ammeter method. Know how to choose between internal and external ammeter connections and assess their errors; keep current controlled to reduce temperature effects on resistivity.",
  ["Can write ρ = πd²R/(4l) and explain how each quantity is measured", "Can choose an internal or external ammeter connection and determine whether the measured value is high or low", "Know error-reduction measures: average repeated measurements, keep current on briefly, and avoid excessive current"],
  [("B3.X.3", "exact"), ("B3.2.2", "exact")],
  pre=[("exp_length", "hard"), ("resistance", "hard"), ("ohm_law", "soft")],
  math=[("cnm_202", "Plot repeated U-I measurements as a linear graph and use its slope to find resistance.")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "A metal wire has resistance of a few ohms. Its ammeter resistance is about 0.1 Ω and its voltmeter resistance is a few thousand ohms. Which voltmeter-ammeter connection should be used, and how does the measured resistance compare with the true value?",
      "external ammeter connection; the measured value is too low", ["external ammeter connection; the measured value is too high", "internal ammeter connection; the measured value is too low", "internal ammeter connection; the measured value is too high"],
      "The unknown resistance is much smaller than the voltmeter's internal resistance, so use the external ammeter connection. The voltmeter draws some current, making the ammeter reading larger than the current through Rx; R_meas = U/I is therefore too low. In the internal connection, the error comes from voltage drop across the ammeter, making R_meas too high; that arrangement is suitable for large resistances."),
    C("basic", "When measuring a metal wire's resistivity, the current should not be too large and should not flow for too long. What is the main reason?",
      "A large current for a long time heats the wire, changing its resistivity and causing error",
      ["a large current for a long time significantly shortens the wire", "a large current for a long time makes the voltmeter reading too low", "a large current for a long time makes the micrometer reading inaccurate"],
      "A metal's resistivity increases with temperature. Joule heating raises the wire's resistance R, so the measured ρ is no longer its room-temperature value. Measuring length and diameter is independent of the current."),
    N("standard", "A metal wire has diameter d = 0.50 mm, effective circuit length l = 1.0 m, and resistance R = 4 Ω. Its resistivity is ρ = πd²R/(4l). Express the result in units of 10⁻⁷ Ω·m, to two decimal places, using π = 3.14159. (Enter a number only.)", "7.85",
      "ρ = πd²R/(4l) = 3.14159 × (0.5×10⁻³)² × 4 ÷ (4 × 1.0) = 7.85×10⁻⁷ Ω·m. Using d as the radius would make the result 4 times too large.",
      "round(3.14159*(0.5e-3)**2*4/(4*1.0)/1e-7,2)"),
    N("advanced", "When measuring resistance by the voltmeter-ammeter method, the ammeter has internal resistance R_A = 1 Ω and the voltmeter has R_V = 900 Ω. Above what unknown resistance, in Ω, does the internal ammeter connection have less error than the external connection? (The threshold is R = √(R_A·R_V). Enter a number only.)", "30",
      "When the relative errors of the internal and external connections are equal, R_x/R_A = R_V/R_x, so R_x = √(R_A R_V) = √(1 × 900) = 30 Ω. For Rx > 30 Ω, R_x/R_A is larger, so the ammeter's voltage-drop effect is smaller and the internal connection is preferred.",
      "sqrt(1*900)"),
  ])

T("series_parallel", "Energy", "procedural",
  "In a series circuit, current is the same everywhere, total voltage is the sum of the individual voltages, and total resistance is R = R₁ + R₂ + … . In a parallel circuit, the voltage across each branch is the same, total current is the sum of branch currents, and 1/R = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ + … . Total parallel resistance is less than the resistance of any branch.",
  ["Can calculate total resistance in series and parallel circuits", "Can use voltage and current division in mixed circuits", "Know that adding parallel branches decreases total resistance, which is dominated by the smallest branch resistance"],
  [("B3.2.3", "exact")],
  pre=[("ohm_law", "hard"), ("J:ohm_sp", "soft"), ("current", "soft")],
  qs=[
    N("basic", "What is the total resistance, in Ω, of 6 Ω and 3 Ω resistors connected in parallel? (Enter a number only.)", "2",
      "1/R = 1/6 + 1/3 = 1/2, so R = 2 Ω. Adding them directly to get 9 Ω would be the series result.",
      "6*3/(6+3)"),
    C("basic", "Which statement about resistance in a parallel circuit is correct?",
      "The total resistance is less than the resistance of any branch, and adding a branch reduces the total resistance",
      ["the total resistance is greater than the largest branch resistance", "adding a branch increases the total resistance", "the total resistance equals the sum of the reciprocals of the branch resistances"],
      "A parallel connection is like increasing the conductor's cross-sectional area, so total resistance is less than that of any branch. The reciprocal of total resistance (1/R), not R itself, is the sum of the branch reciprocals."),
    N("standard", "R₁ = 2 Ω is in series with a parallel combination of R₂ = 6 Ω and R₃ = 3 Ω. The circuit is connected to a 12 V supply. What is the current through R₃, in A? (Enter a number only.)", "2",
      "R₂₃ = 6×3/(6+3) = 2 Ω, total resistance is 4 Ω, and total current is 3 A. The parallel section has voltage U₂₃ = 3 × 2 = 6 V, so I₃ = 6 ÷ 3 = 2 A. Assuming R₃ has the full 12 V across it would give 4 A.",
      "(12/(2+6*3/(6+3)))*(6*3/(6+3))/3"),
    C("standard", "Two resistors, 1 Ω and 99 Ω, are connected in parallel. Which value is closest to the total resistance?",
      "0.99 Ω", ["50 Ω", "100 Ω", "99 Ω"],
      "R = 1 × 99 ÷ (1 + 99) = 0.99 Ω, close to the smaller resistance. 50 Ω is their average, 100 Ω is the series result, and 99 Ω is just the larger resistance.",
      verify="1*99/(1+99)"),
  ])

T("meter_modify", "Energy", "application",
  "A low-range galvanometer (full-scale current I_g, internal resistance R_g) can be converted to a voltmeter by adding a series resistor R = U/I_g − R_g, or to a higher-range ammeter by adding a shunt resistor in parallel, R = I_gR_g/(I − I_g). The conversion increases voltmeter resistance and decreases ammeter resistance.",
  ["Can calculate the series resistance needed to convert a galvanometer to a voltmeter", "Can calculate the parallel resistance needed to convert a galvanometer to an ammeter", "Can analyze how conversion changes meter resistance and explain calibration errors"],
  [("B3.2.3", "inferred"), ("B3.2.1", "inferred")],
  pre=[("series_parallel", "hard"), ("ohm_law", "hard")],
  qs=[
    N("basic", "A galvanometer with full-scale current I_g = 1 mA and internal resistance R_g = 100 Ω is to be converted to a 3 V voltmeter. What series resistor is required, in Ω? (Enter a number only.)", "2900",
      "At full scale, the total resistance is R_total = U/I_g = 3 ÷ 0.001 = 3000 Ω, so the series resistor is R = 3000 − 100 = 2900 Ω. Forgetting to subtract R_g would give 3000 Ω.",
      "3/0.001-100"),
    C("basic", "A low-range ammeter is converted to a higher-range voltmeter and ammeter. Which statement about the methods and internal resistances is correct?",
      "A voltmeter requires a large series resistor, making its resistance greater than the galvanometer's; an ammeter requires a small parallel resistor, making its resistance lower",
      ["a resistor is connected in parallel for a voltmeter and in series for an ammeter", "adding a parallel resistor increases the ammeter's internal resistance", "both converted meters have the same internal resistance as the original galvanometer"],
      "The series voltage-divider resistor makes total voltmeter resistance R_g + R greater than R_g. The parallel shunt gives ammeter resistance R_gR/(R_g + R), less than R_g. A parallel resistor lowers total resistance; it does not increase it."),
    N("standard", "A galvanometer with full-scale current I_g = 10 mA and internal resistance R_g = 90 Ω is to be converted to a 0.1 A ammeter. What shunt resistance is required, in Ω? (Enter a number only.)", "10",
      "The voltage across the galvanometer is U = I_gR_g = 0.01 × 90 = 0.9 V. The shunt current is I − I_g = 0.09 A, so R = 0.9 ÷ 0.09 = 10 Ω. Using 0.1 A as the shunt current would give 9 Ω.",
      "0.01*90/(0.1-0.01)"),
    N("advanced", "A galvanometer with I_g = 1 mA and R_g = 100 Ω is converted to a 3 V voltmeter using a series resistor and checked against a standard voltmeter. When the converted meter reads 2.4 V, the standard meter reads 3.0 V. By how many Ω should the series resistor be reduced to make the converted meter accurate? (Enter a number only.)", "750",
      "The indicated value is proportional to galvanometer current (calibrated for 3000 Ω), and the actual voltage is proportional to total resistance: 3.0 ÷ 2.4 = R_total′ ÷ 3000, giving R_total′ = 3750 Ω and an actual series resistor of 3650 Ω. The correct value is 2900 Ω, so it must be reduced by 750 Ω. A low meter reading means the series resistance is too large.",
      "3000*3.0/2.4-3000"),
  ])

T("exp_multimeter", "Energy", "procedural",
  "A multimeter measures DC voltage, DC current, and resistance. In every mode, current enters the red lead and leaves the black lead. Ohmmeter mode has an internal battery; choose a range and zero the ohmmeter before measuring. Reading = scale reading × multiplier. If the deflection is too small, select a higher multiplier and zero the ohmmeter again after changing ranges.",
  ["Know the relationship between current direction and the red and black multimeter leads", "Can use ohmmeter mode: select a range, zero the meter, and multiply the scale reading by the range", "Can adjust the range based on pointer deflection and use I = E/(R_internal + Rx) to understand how an ohmmeter works"],
  [("B3.X.4", "exact"), ("B3.2.1", "exact")],
  pre=[("meter_modify", "hard"), ("series_parallel", "soft")],
  qs=[
    C("basic", "Which statement correctly describes the direction of current relative to a multimeter's red and black leads?",
      "In voltage, current, and resistance modes, current enters the red lead and leaves the black lead",
      ["current enters red and leaves black only in current mode; the direction is reversed in resistance mode", "in resistance mode, current leaves through red and enters through black", "the red and black leads are interchangeable and current can flow either way"],
      "The galvanometer inside a multimeter requires current to enter in a fixed direction. In ohmmeter mode, the internal battery's positive terminal is connected to the black lead. Current leaves through black, passes through the measured resistor, and enters through red, so it is still “red in, black out” from the meter's perspective."),
    N("basic", "A multimeter is set to the ×100 resistance range, and its pointer is at scale mark 15. What is the measured resistance, in Ω? (Enter a number only.)", "1500",
      "Ohmmeter reading = scale reading × multiplier = 15 × 100 = 1500 Ω. Ohmmeter scales are nonlinear, so read the value at the pointer directly.",
      "15*100"),
    C("standard", "When measuring a resistance in ohmmeter mode, the pointer deflects only a little and is near the ∞ mark. What should be done next?",
      "select a higher multiplier, zero the ohmmeter again, and measure once more", ["select a lower multiplier, zero the ohmmeter again, and measure once more", "select a higher multiplier without re-zeroing", "keep the same multiplier and turn the mechanical-zero screw to move the pointer right"],
      "Small deflection indicates a large resistance and a reading near the high-resistance end, where error is large. Select a higher multiplier to bring the pointer closer to the middle of the scale. Changing ranges changes the internal equivalent circuit, so the ohmmeter must be zeroed again. Mechanical zero only adjusts the pointer's position before measurement."),
    N("standard", "An ohmmeter has battery emf E = 1.5 V and total internal resistance 1500 Ω after zeroing. What current, in mA, flows through its galvanometer when a 4500 Ω resistor Rx is connected? (Enter a number only.)", "0.25",
      "I = E/(R_internal + Rx) = 1.5 ÷ (1500 + 4500) = 2.5×10⁻⁴ A = 0.25 mA. At zero resistance, full-scale current is I_g = E/R_internal = 1 mA, so the pointer now deflects to 1/4 of full scale.",
      "1.5/(1500+4500)*1000"),
  ])
